detail.abstract:
This paper investigates the paradoxical role of High-Frequency Trading (HFT) in modern financial markets, which have transitioned from human-intermediated "Mediocristan" to algorithmic "Extremistan." While HFT demonstrably reduces transaction costs and improves local efficiency during tranquil periods, this study, utilizing granular Level 3 (L3) ITCH data, isolates a causal link between HFT participation and systemic fragility.&nbsp;
<div>
 The core finding establishes a definitive "saturation point" beyond which the marginal benefit of algorithmic liquidity provision follows a path of negative convexity— beneficial at the mean but catastrophic in the tails.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 &nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Through a rigorous decomposition of realized volatility, the research confirms that HFT agents are "stabilizing until they aren't," primarily by withdrawing liquidity during macro shocks (the "liquidity vacuum") and amplifying jump risk above a critical participation threshold. The paper also extends its analysis to multi-sectoral perspectives, including the 24/7 algorithmic intensity of cryptocurrency and the rise of "Information Finance" (InfoFi) in prediction markets. Finally, applying the Taleb Heuristic, the study concludes that HFT, by possessing "free exit" and lacking "Skin in the Game," increases the market's mechanical efficiency at the expense of its organic resilience. Strategic recommendations are proposed for investors, regulators, and market designers to encourage a transition from brittle, glass-like systems to an antifragile financial architecture.
</div>
